const units=[
{id:'dom-safe-content',comp:'m1.c1',title:'Select nodes and insert text without interpreting markup',introduction:'A support portal displays customer-written ticket titles. The title should appear as text even if it contains angle brackets. Selecting the right node and choosing the right insertion API is part of correctness and privacy, not merely presentation.',sections:[s('The DOM is a live object interface','The document object model represents nodes and their relationships. querySelector returns the first matching element or null; querySelectorAll returns a static NodeList for its selected matches. Some other collections are live, so check the API rather than assuming every collection updates identically. A selector identifies nodes, not an authorization policy.','Use createElement to create a node and textContent to set text when markup interpretation is not intended. Setting innerHTML parses a string as markup and is an unsafe sink for untrusted text without an appropriate reviewed sanitization policy. Attribute and URL destinations also need context-specific validation; textContent is not a universal policy for every destination.'),s('Handle missing nodes and preserve intended structure','Check a possibly absent selection before using it, or make the existence requirement explicit at the component boundary. Replacing a container’s textContent removes its existing child nodes; it does not simply edit one nested text fragment. Use append or replaceChildren deliberately and consider focus when replacing active controls.','The Node fixture constructs a text-only view model and serializes it as JSON. It deliberately does not implement or execute a browser DOM. Real selector, insertion, focus and markup behavior requires the separate browser regression evidence; the lesson’s API guidance must not be confused with this model’s narrower output.')],worked:w('const title = "<em>Delivery update</em>";\nconst view = {nodeType: "text", value: title};\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(view));\nconsole.log(view.value === title);','{"nodeType":"text","value":"<em>Delivery update</em>"}\ntrue','Preserve synthetic markup-shaped content as an intended text value.',['Declare the customer title as data.','Construct a text-intent view model rather than a markup interpreter.','Verify the exact data was preserved.'],'This fixture is not a DOM engine and does not prove browser textContent behavior. Actual browser tests are required for the API boundary.'),mistakes:['Assigning untrusted strings to innerHTML for ordinary text.','Assuming querySelector can never return null.'],takeaways:['Choose insertion APIs according to interpretation context.','Separate browser evidence from text-intent models.'],quiz:[
['What can querySelector return when no element matches?','null','Always a new div','Always an empty array','A guaranteed button','The API can report no matching element with null.','It does not create a replacement element.','Its result is not an array.','Matching is not guaranteed.'],
['Which API is suited to ordinary untrusted text in a node?','textContent','Unreviewed innerHTML','A generated script string','A copied inline event handler','Text content avoids interpreting the value as HTML markup in that text-setting context.','InnerHTML interprets markup.','Script construction adds a different unsafe interpreter context.','Event-handler code is not text display.'],
['What does innerHTML do with its string?','Parses markup','Only stores a plain text label','Validates account permission','Encrypts the content','InnerHTML is a markup interpretation sink rather than a general text-only API.','Plain text insertion has different semantics.','DOM parsing is not server authorization.','No encryption is implied.'],
['A container’s textContent is replaced. What can happen to its children?','They are replaced by the text','They are all preserved unchanged','They become server records','They are automatically focused','Setting textContent replaces the relevant child content rather than selectively preserving nested controls.','That is not a universal preservation guarantee.','No server storage occurs.','Focus behavior needs separate handling.'],
['A customer title contains <strong>. The requirement is literal text. What should the implementation avoid?','Unnecessary markup parsing','A deliberate text value','Testing the browser result','A clear rendering contract','Interpreting customer text as markup exceeds the stated literal-text requirement and can create risk.','Text data is appropriate.','Browser testing is necessary.','The contract helps choose the right API.'],
['A selected node is absent after a route change. What should be handled?','The missing-element state','Only the selector’s font','Only a cookie name','Only the source file size','Route/lifecycle changes can mean the selected element does not exist at that moment.','Fonts do not create selected nodes.','Cookies do not guarantee the DOM target.','File size does not handle null.'],
['An element selection succeeds. What does it not grant?','Server permission','A node reference','The selected element identity','Access to its allowed DOM API','Finding a DOM node does not authorize a protected backend operation.','That is the selection result.','The reference identifies the node.','DOM interaction follows browser capabilities.'],
['The fixture prints a text-intent JSON object. What remains unverified?','Actual browser insertion','The supplied title string','The view model’s value','The equality comparison','A model with nodeType=text is not a browser DOM or textContent test.','The string is explicit input.','The value is direct model state.','The comparison is executed.']
]},
{id:'dom-event-listeners',comp:'m1.c2',title:'Register event listeners with clear lifecycle ownership',introduction:'A registration page mounts and unmounts a save panel. If each mount adds another listener without removing the old one, one click can trigger duplicate work even though the button looks normal.',sections:[s('An event listener belongs to a target and lifecycle','addEventListener registers a callback for an event type on an event target. The handler receives an event object with information about the interaction. currentTarget identifies the target on which that listener is registered during handling; target identifies the event’s originating target as exposed through the event model, with shadow-DOM retargeting details where relevant.','Removing a listener requires the appropriate target, type, callback identity and matching capture flag. Recreating a syntactically identical anonymous function does not reuse the same function identity. Options such as once or an AbortSignal can support deliberate lifecycle control; do not assume they handle every application state automatically.'),s('Keep listener cleanup separate from operation correctness','Use preventDefault only when intentionally replacing the default behavior; it does not stop propagation. stopPropagation affects propagation but is not a server permission check. Duplicate-submit protection must also consider pending state and backend idempotency where the operation requires it.','The fixture uses Node’s EventTarget and Event to execute actual listener identity, once and removal behavior. It does not run browser click defaults, DOM propagation, focus or shadow-DOM rules. Browser-specific event claims still need a rendered interface test.')],worked:w('const target = new EventTarget();\nlet calls = 0;\nconst handler = () => { calls += 1; };\ntarget.addEventListener("save", handler);\ntarget.dispatchEvent(new Event("save"));\ntarget.removeEventListener("save", handler);\ntarget.dispatchEvent(new Event("save"));\ntarget.addEventListener("save", handler, {once: true});\ntarget.dispatchEvent(new Event("save"));\ntarget.dispatchEvent(new Event("save"));\nconsole.log(calls);','2','Observe actual EventTarget listener removal and once handling under Node.',['Register a known callback identity.','Dispatch before and after removing that callback.','Register once and compare two later dispatches.'],'This checks Node’s EventTarget API behavior, not browser DOM bubbling, native form defaults or keyboard interaction.'),mistakes:['Removing a listener with a newly created anonymous function.','Treating preventDefault as propagation control or server authorization.'],takeaways:['Own listener registration and cleanup together.','Distinguish event behavior from duplicate-operation protection.'],quiz:[
['What identity matters when removing a listener?','The registered callback function','Only equivalent function text','Only a matching local name string','Only the HTML tag','A new function with the same body is a different function value from the registered callback.','Textual similarity is not object identity.','Names alone do not identify the function object.','The tag does not replace the callback argument.'],
['What does once:true request?','One listener invocation before removal','One global click forever','One server transaction automatically','One browser page load','The option makes that listener registration automatically remove after its first invocation.','It does not disable all other clicks.','Server operations remain separate.','Page loading is a different lifecycle.'],
['What does preventDefault affect?','The default action when cancelable','Every propagation phase automatically','The server’s permission role','The database schema','PreventDefault requests cancellation of a cancelable event’s default action.','Stopping propagation is different.','Client events do not grant server authority.','No schema change is implied.'],
['A panel adds another save listener on every mount. What can happen?','Duplicate handler work','Automatic deep state isolation','Guaranteed request deduplication','Automatic database rollback','Multiple active listeners can make one interaction run work more than once.','Listeners can still share state.','Deduplication is not automatic.','Rollback is not supplied by event registration.'],
['An anonymous removal callback has the same source text as the registered one. Why can removal fail?','It is a different function object','Its text is too short','The event name is always ignored','The browser cannot run anonymous functions','Listener matching depends on function identity, not merely source appearance.','Length is not the criterion.','Event type is part of registration matching.','Anonymous functions are valid.'],
['A handler calls stopPropagation. What remains independent?','Server operation authorization','The event’s local handling','The chosen propagation policy','The handler’s code execution','Propagation control does not establish permission for a backend mutation.','Local handling is part of the event behavior.','That is the API’s intended concern.','The handler still executes.'],
['A listener should be removed when a feature is disposed. What can help?','An owned AbortSignal or cleanup','Only a CSS display change','Only a shorter button label','Only a new domain','Lifecycle-bound cancellation or explicit removal can clean up the registration.','Hiding a node does not universally remove all listeners.','Labels do not dispose callbacks.','Domains are unrelated.'],
['The EventTarget fixture prints 2. What is outside its scope?','DOM bubbling and form defaults','The callback count','The once option invocation','The explicit removal behavior','Node EventTarget is not a full browser DOM with native interactive defaults.','The count is printed.','The registration is executed.','Removal is directly exercised.']
]},
{id:'dom-event-delegation',comp:'m1.c2',title:'Delegate dynamic-list events without acting on the wrong item',introduction:'A support ticket list adds rows after data arrives. A single listener on the list can handle buttons in those rows, but it must distinguish the intended action from clicks on nested icons or unrelated elements.',sections:[s('Use propagation deliberately','In a normal light-DOM bubbling case, a listener on an ancestor can observe events from descendants. event.target may be a nested icon while currentTarget is the list listener’s element. Use a deliberate matching strategy such as target.closest("button[data-action]") and verify that the matched element belongs to the intended container.','Not every event bubbles, and shadow-DOM composed paths and retargeting add details beyond this introductory model. Delegation is a technique, not a reason to ignore an API’s event propagation rules. A data attribute can describe a requested action or record identifier but remains client-side data, not proof of permission.'),s('Validate the action and preserve interaction quality','Allowlist recognized actions instead of calling arbitrary property names from the DOM. Resolve the current record through the application’s data contract, manage pending/error feedback and verify server authorization independently. Avoid making the entire row unexpectedly activate when a user intended a different control.','The fixture walks a supplied ancestor chain to find an allowlisted button action. It is explicitly a model of matching logic, not an executed DOM closest/bubbling test. Browser tests must cover nested targets, dynamic rows, container ownership and keyboard activation.')],worked:w('const path = [{tag: "svg"}, {tag: "button", action: "close", ticket: 7}, {tag: "li"}, {tag: "ul"}];\nconst allowed = new Set(["close", "reopen"]);\nconst control = path.find(node => node.tag === "button" && allowed.has(node.action));\nconsole.log(control.action, control.ticket);\nconsole.log(allowed.has("delete-everything"));','close 7\nfalse','Resolve one intended action from a synthetic nested target path.',['Read the closest-to-farthest path records.','Find a button with a recognized action.','Reject an unrelated action name.'],'The path is supplied test data. This is not browser propagation, shadow-DOM retargeting or server authorization evidence.'),mistakes:['Assuming every click target is already the button.','Treating a data-id as authoritative record ownership.'],takeaways:['Resolve the intended control and confirm container scope.','Keep action allowlists and server permissions explicit.'],quiz:[
['In delegated handling, what can target represent?','A nested originating element','Always the listener container','Always the document root','Only the server account','The event target can be a descendant such as an icon rather than the ancestor with the listener.','CurrentTarget identifies the listener’s registration target during handling.','The root is not universal.','Events do not identify server authority by themselves.'],
['What does currentTarget identify during a listener call?','The listener’s registration target','Always the deepest clicked node','The newest API response','The user’s database row','CurrentTarget is the event target whose listener is currently being invoked.','Target can be the originating descendant.','Responses are separate.','Client events do not select authoritative rows.'],
['Do all event types bubble?','No, inspect the event contract','Yes, without exceptions','Only if their name is short','Only when CSS is absent','Delegation must respect the particular event’s propagation behavior.','A universal bubbling claim is wrong.','Name length is irrelevant.','CSS presence does not define bubbling.'],
['A user clicks an svg inside a close button. What should delegated logic resolve?','The ancestor control','Only the svg tag name','A random sibling row','Every button on the page','A closest/matching strategy can find the intended actionable ancestor.','The icon is not necessarily the operation owner.','Sibling actions can affect the wrong record.','Broad activation exceeds the one interaction.'],
['A matched action button is outside the intended list container. What is needed?','A container ownership check','Only a darker icon','Only a longer action string','Only a new event name','Delegated matching should not escape the component boundary unintentionally.','Colour does not enforce scope.','Length does not establish membership.','Renaming alone does not verify ancestry.'],
['A data-action contains an unknown method name. What should happen?','Reject it under an allowlist','Invoke any matching global function','Treat it as a verified permission','Send every record to the client','Action data should be constrained to intended supported operations.','Arbitrary dispatch broadens risk.','DOM data is not authority.','Excessive disclosure is unrelated and harmful.'],
['A dynamically appended row should use the list’s delegated handler. What must still be tested?','Real propagation and activation','Only static source inspection','Only one old row screenshot','Only the new row’s text length','Dynamic DOM behavior and keyboard/mouse interaction need actual browser evidence.','Source alone does not run events.','An old screenshot misses the new path.','Length does not establish propagation.'],
['The path model finds close ticket 7. What did it not prove?','Server permission for ticket 7','The supplied path order','The recognized action match','The rejected unknown action','A local matching model cannot authorize the backend operation.','Order is explicit input.','The match is executed.','The set rejects the supplied unknown string.']
]},
{id:'async-sync-timer-order',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Separate synchronous execution from timer tasks',introduction:'A dashboard schedules a refresh with a zero-delay timer and immediately updates its status text. Zero delay does not mean the timer interrupts the current JavaScript execution. Predicting order starts with run-to-completion and the relevant scheduling queues.',sections:[s('Finish the current synchronous work','Ordinary JavaScript statements in the current task run in order until that execution yields or completes. A timer schedules a later callback; a zero delay is not a promise of exact immediate execution or a precise elapsed-time deadline. Actual delay can be affected by load, runtime policy and browser throttling.','A long synchronous loop can delay user interaction and scheduled callbacks on the same event-loop thread. Asynchronous scheduling does not automatically move CPU work to another thread. Workers and other concurrency mechanisms have explicit boundaries and communication rules.'),s('State the runtime before claiming a universal trace','Browser event loops and Node scheduling share useful concepts but differ in details such as Node-specific phases and APIs. Do not generalize a setTimeout/setImmediate ordering claim to every context without testing and documenting it. The example uses only an unambiguous current-stack-versus-timer sequence.','The fixture runs actual Node timers and synchronous statements. It confirms this declared language/runtime order without measuring latency or testing browser rendering opportunities. Browser responsiveness and throttling require browser-level evidence.')],worked:w('console.log("start");\nsetTimeout(() => console.log("timer"), 0);\nconsole.log("end");','start\nend\ntimer','Predict current synchronous output before a scheduled zero-delay timer.',['Print the first synchronous marker.','Schedule the callback without invoking it inline.','Finish synchronous work before the timer can run.'],'The test establishes output order for this runtime case, not exact timer latency or every Node/browser queue ordering.'),mistakes:['Reading zero delay as interruption of the current statement sequence.','Assuming asynchronous scheduling makes heavy CPU work parallel.'],takeaways:['Follow the current stack before later callbacks.','State the runtime and avoid unsupported timing guarantees.'],quiz:[
['Does setTimeout(callback,0) call the callback inline?','No, it schedules later work','Yes, before the next statement','Only when the callback is short','Only when the variable is const','Timers schedule callbacks rather than synchronously invoking them at the registration expression.','The current synchronous path continues.','Body length does not make it inline.','Binding kind does not change timer scheduling.'],
['What can a long synchronous loop delay?','Other queued work','Only CSS filenames','Only Git commits','Only DNS record ownership','Run-to-completion can prevent other callbacks and interactions on the same thread until yielding.','Filenames are not scheduled interactions.','Repository history is unrelated.','Ownership is not event-loop work.'],
['What does zero timer delay not guarantee?','Exact immediate execution','That the callback is registered','That later scheduling is requested','That the callback is a function value','Actual execution waits for the runtime’s scheduling conditions and can be delayed.','Registration can occur normally.','That is the API’s intended scheduling request.','The callback can be a supplied function.'],
['A refresh timer is scheduled then status is set synchronously. Which occurs first in this simple case?','The synchronous status update','The timer interrupts it','Both always execute in parallel','The browser must restart','The current synchronous path completes before the scheduled callback runs.','The timer is not an interrupt.','Ordinary callbacks do not imply parallel execution.','No restart is required.'],
['A team wraps expensive computation in a timer and claims it runs off-thread. What is wrong?','Scheduling is not parallelism','All timers are forbidden','The computation is now validated','Every object is cloned','A later callback can still occupy the same event-loop thread.','Timers are valid tools.','Scheduling does not check correctness.','No automatic cloning occurs.'],
['A timer fires later in a background tab. What may need consideration?','Runtime throttling and load','Only variable name length','Only the page’s favicon','Only JSON indentation','Browsers can throttle background work and timers are not precise deadlines.','Names do not control scheduling policy.','Icons do not define delay.','Formatting does not choose timing.'],
['Can one Node timer example certify browser paint timing?','No, rendering is separate evidence','Yes, all loops are identical','Only if console output is short','Only when HTTPS is used','Node output does not execute a browser renderer or establish its paint opportunities.','Runtime details differ.','Output length is not renderer evidence.','Transport does not determine paint scheduling.'],
['The example prints start/end/timer. What was observed?','A scoped output order','A measured zero-millisecond delay','Every callback API’s order','A universal worker policy','The fixture checks relative ordering only for the declared operations.','No precise latency was measured.','Other APIs can have different rules.','Workers were not tested.']
]},
{id:'async-microtask-order',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Trace promise reactions and microtasks',introduction:'A save panel starts a resolved-promise continuation and a timer. Both are asynchronous, but they do not necessarily enter the same scheduling queue. Distinguishing the queues explains why a promise callback can run before a timer callback.',sections:[s('Promise reactions wait for the current stack','A promise represents an eventual result, with pending, fulfilled or rejected state. A then callback is scheduled as a reaction rather than called synchronously merely because the promise is already fulfilled. Queueing a microtask also schedules work after the current synchronous execution.','In the common browser model, microtasks are processed at checkpoints before the next task, with important details in the event-loop specification. Continuously adding microtasks can starve later tasks and rendering opportunities. Promises do not turn CPU work into a separate thread.'),s('Use a deliberately unambiguous trace','The example schedules a fulfilled-promise reaction, queueMicrotask and a timer while printing synchronous markers. Under the declared Node runtime this sequence has a stable observed order; Node-only nextTick and phase-sensitive APIs are deliberately absent.','The output tests queue relationships in this example. It is not a performance benchmark, a guarantee about exact wall-clock time or evidence that browsers render between every promise callback. Use browser traces when responsiveness is the actual question.')],worked:w('console.log("A");\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log("promise"));\nqueueMicrotask(() => console.log("microtask"));\nsetTimeout(() => console.log("timer"), 0);\nconsole.log("B");','A\nB\npromise\nmicrotask\ntimer','Trace synchronous markers, queued microtasks and a timer.',['Read all scheduling calls without invoking callbacks inline.','Finish the synchronous A/B path.','Follow the queued promise reaction and microtask before the timer.'],'This is actual runtime execution for the supplied APIs, not a universal trace for every browser/Node scheduling primitive.'),mistakes:['Calling an already-fulfilled promise reaction synchronous.','Creating an unbounded microtask chain that prevents useful yielding.'],takeaways:['Promise state and callback scheduling are different.','Avoid confusing asynchronous queues with parallel threads.'],quiz:[
['Is a then callback on Promise.resolve invoked inline?','No, the reaction is queued','Yes, before then returns always','Only if the value is a string','Only when const is used','Promise reactions are scheduled rather than directly invoked inline by then.','Synchronous invocation is not the rule.','Payload type does not choose inline behavior.','Binding kind is unrelated.'],
['Which state can a promise have?','Three settlement states','Only true or false','Only locked or deleted','Only DOM-ready','Promises represent eventual settlement through their defined states.','Booleans are values, not the complete state set.','Those are not the general promise states.','DOM readiness is a different event.'],
['What can queueMicrotask schedule?','A microtask callback','A guaranteed worker thread','A database transaction','A CSS media query','QueueMicrotask schedules a callback in the runtime’s microtask mechanism.','It does not create a worker.','Transactions are separate.','CSS queries are not callbacks in this API.'],
['A fulfilled promise is created during synchronous work. When does its then handler run in the shown case?','After that synchronous work','Before its registration statement ends','On a separate CPU by guarantee','Only after every future timer','The reaction is queued and the current synchronous execution finishes first.','It is not an inline call.','No separate CPU is promised.','The ordering is not after all future timers.'],
['A feature repeatedly queues more microtasks without bound. What is a risk?','Starvation of later work','Guaranteed smoother rendering','Automatic request cancellation','Automatic memory cleanup','An unbounded microtask chain can prevent progress to other tasks and rendering opportunities.','It may harm responsiveness.','Cancellation is not automatic.','Reachable work can continue retaining data.'],
['A developer equates promises with parallel CPU execution. What is the correction?','It need not create threads','Every promise is invalid','Only arrays can be asynchronous','All code becomes synchronous','Promise reactions can still execute on the same event-loop thread.','Promises are valid language mechanisms.','Arrays do not define asynchronous capability.','Queued reactions remain asynchronous.'],
['A page must render before heavy work continues. What is insufficient evidence?','Only a resolved-promise trace','A browser performance recording','A measured interaction test','A clear runtime contract','A promise microtask does not by itself establish a browser rendering opportunity.','That can provide relevant evidence.','That tests the actual concern.','Stating the contract helps scope claims.'],
['The fixture prints promise before microtask. What does that reflect here?','Their queued registration order','A universal promise priority rule','The string length of the labels','A server permission decision','Both were queued in that order in this declared trace.','Other scheduling contexts require their own analysis.','Labels do not choose scheduling.','No server authority is involved.']
]},
{id:'async-yield-budget',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Protect responsiveness with bounded scheduled work',introduction:'A reporting page processes a large local dataset. Making a function async does not automatically prevent long blocking sections: a single callback can still monopolize the event loop unless the design limits work and yields appropriately.',sections:[s('Identify the actual blocking segment','An async function executes synchronously until it reaches a suspension point such as await. Code before that point can still be expensive. Awaiting an already-fulfilled promise yields to promise continuation scheduling, which is not a universal guarantee of an intervening paint or user-input task.','Break work into bounded units where appropriate, or use a worker when the workload and transfer costs justify a separate execution context. Define cancellation and stale-result handling so an old computation does not overwrite a newly selected report. A generation identifier can help ignore obsolete results, but it is not a server permission check.'),s('Do not claim timing from counters','The fixture demonstrates generation-based acceptance of one result and rejection of an older one. It tests a small state policy, not a browser scheduler, actual yielding or worker performance. Production batch size should be based on measured responsiveness rather than an arbitrary item count.','Keep progress honest: a scheduled chunk is not completed work, and a cancelled view does not necessarily undo a remote side effect. Coordinate UI cancellation with the actual operation contract and maintain useful safe diagnostics.')],worked:w('let currentGeneration = 2;\nfunction acceptResult(generation, value) { return generation === currentGeneration ? value : "stale ignored"; }\nconsole.log(acceptResult(1, "old report"));\nconsole.log(acceptResult(2, "new report"));\nconsole.log("measure real task duration separately");','stale ignored\nnew report\nmeasure real task duration separately','Reject an obsolete result without pretending the model measures responsiveness.',['Declare the currently selected generation.','Compare an older result generation.','Accept only the matching generation’s value.'],'The policy prevents this modeled stale overwrite. It does not execute chunking, rendering, workers or remote cancellation.'),mistakes:['Assuming async makes all code nonblocking.','Showing scheduled work as completed progress.'],takeaways:['Measure the blocking path and bound its work.','Separate stale-result policy from scheduling and remote-effect guarantees.'],quiz:[
['Does an async function’s initial code always run on a worker?','No, not by that keyword','Yes, a thread is created','Only if it returns a number','Only on small screens','Async changes return/suspension behavior, not an automatic execution-thread assignment.','No worker is created by default.','Result type does not choose threads.','Viewport size is irrelevant.'],
['What should determine a useful processing chunk budget?','Measured responsiveness','Only a round item count','Only the filename length','Only the page colour','Actual workload and interaction measurements are more useful than arbitrary counts.','Counts can have very different costs.','Names do not measure duration.','Colours do not measure blocking.'],
['What can a generation identifier help reject?','Obsolete local results','Every unauthorized server request','Every duplicate financial effect','Every memory allocation','A current-generation comparison can prevent stale results from replacing a newer view.','Permissions require server policy.','Remote side effects need their own contract.','Allocation is not controlled universally.'],
['A user changes report filters before an old calculation finishes. What should completion handling verify?','Whether the result is still current','Only whether the old array was nonempty','Only the title’s font size','Only the route’s string length','An old result can be valid for an obsolete selection and should not overwrite the new state.','Nonempty does not mean current.','Fonts do not identify result generation.','Length does not determine relevance.'],
['A UI says 100% when all chunks were only queued. What is wrong?','Scheduling is not completion','Every chunk already succeeded','The browser validated all data','The worker saved a database','Queued work is not evidence that processing or effects finished.','No completion result is established.','Scheduling does not validate data.','No storage evidence is supplied.'],
['A promise continuation is used as the only proof of a paint boundary. What is missing?','Actual browser scheduling evidence','Only a longer promise variable','Only a new CSS class','Only an extra JSON key','Microtask continuation does not guarantee a rendering opportunity in every intended context.','Naming does not create evidence.','Styling hooks do not establish scheduling.','Keys do not measure paint.'],
['A computation’s view is cancelled after a remote mutation began. What should not be assumed?','The remote effect was undone','The view can stop displaying it','A stale local result can be ignored','The operation needs a defined policy','Local cancellation and remote effect reversal are different contracts.','Local presentation can change.','A generation policy can ignore a result.','Defining semantics is necessary.'],
['The generation model accepts new report. What remains unmeasured?','Real task duration and rendering','The supplied generation equality','The ignored stale value','The returned current value','The fixture is a result-selection policy rather than a performance or browser scheduler test.','That comparison is executed.','That rejection is observed.','That value is printed.']
]},
{id:'async-await-flow',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Read async functions as promise-producing control flow',introduction:'A dashboard loads a customer summary and formats it for display. Async/await can make control flow easier to read, but it does not turn an asynchronous result into an immediately available value for every caller.',sections:[s('An async call returns a promise','An async function always returns a promise. Returning a plain value fulfills that promise with the value; throwing causes rejection. Await suspends the async function’s continuation until the awaited value is resolved through promise semantics, while other runtime work can proceed according to scheduling rules.','The caller must await or otherwise handle that promise to use the eventual value. Logging the promise object is not the same as observing its fulfillment result. Awaiting a nonpromise value still uses asynchronous continuation semantics; do not use it as a universal browser paint guarantee.'),s('Keep sequencing and failure paths explicit','When the second request depends on the first result, await the dependency in order. When operations are independent, serial awaiting can add unnecessary latency, but parallelization also needs capacity and failure policy. Put the loading, success, empty and error states in the actual UI contract instead of inventing data after a rejection.','The fixture uses resolved synthetic values and records the order around an await. It executes actual JavaScript promise behavior but no external request or browser view. Network reliability and interface rendering require separate tests.')],worked:w('async function summary() { console.log("inside"); const value = await Promise.resolve("ready"); return value.toUpperCase(); }\nconst pending = summary();\nconsole.log("caller continues");\npending.then(value => console.log(value));','inside\ncaller continues\nREADY','Trace the caller’s continuation and an async function’s eventual result.',['Start the async function and observe its initial synchronous marker.','Read the caller’s next statement while the result is pending.','Observe the fulfilled value through then.'],'The example has no network delay and establishes order rather than a latency estimate or browser rendering result.'),mistakes:['Treating an async return as an immediate plain value.','Adding await everywhere without analyzing dependencies.'],takeaways:['An async call returns a promise.','State sequencing and error behavior deliberately.'],quiz:[
['What does an async function call return?','A promise','Always a plain string','Always a DOM node','Always undefined','Async functions wrap their result in promise semantics.','A returned string becomes a fulfillment value.','DOM nodes are not an automatic result.','The promise itself is the call result.'],
['What does an uncaught throw inside an async function produce?','A rejected promise','A guaranteed fulfilled zero','A browser reload always','A CSS warning only','The async function’s promise rejects when its execution throws.','Rejection is not successful zero.','Reload behavior is not implied.','This is language error behavior.'],
['What does await suspend?','That async continuation','Every CPU in the system','Every other user’s browser','All server permissions','Await suspends the relevant function continuation rather than universally freezing all work.','It does not stop every processor.','Other users are unrelated.','Permissions are a separate concern.'],
['A caller stores fetchSummary() without awaiting it. What should it handle?','The returned promise','A guaranteed completed summary','A deep copied customer always','A browser screen directly','The eventual value must be observed through promise handling.','Completion has not been established.','No copy is automatic.','A promise is not a rendered screen.'],
['A second operation requires the first operation’s identifier. What execution style fits?','Explicit dependent sequencing','Start both with a missing identifier','Ignore the first error','Use random placeholder ids','The dependency must be resolved before using the resulting identifier.','Parallel start cannot invent needed input.','Ignoring failure loses correctness.','Random IDs are not the actual result.'],
['Two independent reads are awaited one after another. What should be considered?','Concurrency suitability','Only the variable names','Only the code indentation','Only the page’s logo','Independent operations can sometimes overlap, subject to capacity and failure policy.','Names do not determine dependency.','Formatting does not change the contract.','Logos do not choose concurrency.'],
['A rejected load is replaced with fake customer success data. What is the issue?','The UI misstates evidence','Await repaired the failure','The promise became authoritative','The server verified the fake data','Error handling must not invent a successful business result.','Await does not repair arbitrary failure.','A fabricated value is not evidence.','No server verification occurred.'],
['The fixture prints caller continues before READY. What does it demonstrate?','The await continuation occurs later','A guaranteed network request occurred','A worker thread was created','A browser paint was measured','The async function resumes through scheduled promise continuation after the current synchronous path.','The values are synthetic local promises.','No worker is created.','No renderer is present.']
]},
{id:'async-rejection-cleanup',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Handle rejections and cleanup without hiding failures',introduction:'A settings panel must stop showing “saving” whether a save succeeds or fails. A finally block can restore local UI state, while the success/error message must still reflect the actual operation result.',sections:[s('Catch the promise path you actually await','Try/catch around an awaited promise can handle its rejection. Starting a promise without awaiting or returning it can detach the failure from the intended caller. A handler should either recover according to a defined policy or preserve/rethrow an error with useful context. Do not use an empty catch to make monitoring look green.','Finally can reset a local pending flag, but cleanup failure itself needs a policy. Returning or throwing inside finally can override an earlier outcome. A loading flag returning to false means local pending presentation ended; it does not mean that a remote operation committed or rolled back.'),s('Keep diagnostics safe and user states honest','Show a useful safe message for expected failures and record appropriate nonsecret diagnostics privately. Error objects can contain request or payload context, so avoid dumping raw credentials and private records into user-visible output. Distinguish abort, validation, authorization and unexpected failures where the application contract needs them.','The worked fixture rejects a synthetic save promise and resets a local flag. It proves this language/control-flow branch only, not a remote provider’s rollback or browser focus/announcement behavior.')],worked:w('(async () => {\n  let pending = true;\n  try { await Promise.reject(new Error("synthetic save failed")); }\n  catch (error) { console.log(error.message); }\n  finally { pending = false; }\n  console.log("pending:", pending);\n})().catch(error => { console.error(error.message); process.exitCode = 1; });','synthetic save failed\npending: false','Handle a rejected synthetic save and end its local pending state.',['Await the promise inside the protected try.','Observe the rejection through catch.','Reset the local presentation flag in finally.'],'The false flag is cleanup evidence only. No remote mutation, rollback or rendered error announcement is tested.'),mistakes:['Launching a promise without preserving its error path.','Calling a cleared loading flag proof of business success.'],takeaways:['Attach handlers to the actual asynchronous path.','Separate cleanup, recovery and outcome evidence.'],quiz:[
['What lets try/catch handle a promise rejection here?','Awaiting it inside try','Only naming the promise safe','Only starting it before try','Only hiding the button','The awaited rejection enters that async function’s protected error path.','Names do not attach handlers.','Detached work may fail outside the intended boundary.','Presentation does not handle a promise.'],
['What can finally reset appropriately?','A local pending flag','Every server transaction automatically','Every session globally','Every database backup','Finally can implement deliberate local cleanup such as ending a pending display state.','Rollback needs actual storage semantics.','Session revocation is separate.','Backups are not automatic cleanup.'],
['What should an empty catch not be used to claim?','That the operation succeeded','That an error was intercepted','That monitoring needs policy','That recovery needs design','Silently ignoring an error is not success evidence.','A catch can intercept an error.','Monitoring policy remains necessary.','Recovery is a deliberate choice.'],
['A save promise starts but is neither awaited nor returned. What can be lost?','Failure propagation','The function’s source text','All lexical scope','The HTML title','Detached work can reject outside the caller’s intended error-handling chain.','Source remains available.','Closures can still exist.','Titles are unrelated.'],
['A finally return replaces an earlier rejection. What should be reviewed?','The overridden outcome','Only the string’s colour','Only the branch name','Only the page width','Finally control flow can suppress or replace an earlier result or error.','Colour does not define promise semantics.','Repository branches do not choose that flow.','Viewport width is unrelated.'],
['An error object includes a raw access token. What must diagnostics consider?','Sensitive-data minimization','Only JSON indentation','Only a longer error name','Only printing it to users faster','Logging/response handling must protect credentials even during failures.','Whitespace does not remove secret data.','Names do not sanitize content.','Faster exposure is still exposure.'],
['A pending flag becomes false after rejection. What does that indicate?','Local cleanup completed','The server definitely committed','The server definitely rolled back','The user gained permission','The flag tracks local presentation, not authoritative remote transaction state.','No commit evidence is supplied.','No rollback evidence is supplied.','Cleanup does not grant rights.'],
['The synthetic rejection fixture passes. What remains to test in a real UI?','Rendered error recovery','The explicit false assignment','The supplied rejection message','The catch branch output','Language control-flow execution does not prove useful browser focus, messaging or accessibility behavior.','The assignment is executed.','The message is supplied fixture data.','That output is observed.']
]},
{id:'async-parallel-contracts',comp:'m3.c2',title:'Choose parallel or sequential work with a failure policy',introduction:'A dashboard needs a profile, a preference summary and a report count. Independent reads can start together, while a second operation requiring a created identifier must wait. The choice is about dependencies and capacity, not an unconditional “parallel is faster” rule.',sections:[s('Start independent promises intentionally','Promise.all waits for all input promises to fulfill and returns values in input order, not completion order. It rejects when an input rejects, but does not automatically cancel other already-started operations. Starting thousands of requests at once can overload the service, so use an appropriate concurrency limit and deadline policy.','Promise.allSettled reports each settlement when partial results are useful. That does not mean every failure can be ignored; the UI must explain which information is unavailable and whether an action remains safe. Sequential await is appropriate when later input depends on an earlier result or when ordered effects are required.'),s('Handle overlap and repeated work','A race between old and new requests can display stale data unless completion checks relevance. Aborting an obsolete fetch can reduce unnecessary work, but cancellation and remote effects have their own semantics. Retrying a state-changing operation needs idempotency and uncertainty handling, not just repeating it after every timeout.','The fixture uses delayed synthetic promises with deliberately different completion timing and records Promise.all’s input-order result. It does not benchmark network latency, test provider limits or prove cancellation of outstanding requests.')],worked:w('(async () => {\n  const slow = new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve("profile"), 5));\n  const fast = Promise.resolve("count");\n  const values = await Promise.all([slow, fast]);\n  console.log(JSON.stringify(values));\n  const outcomes = await Promise.allSettled([Promise.resolve("ok"), Promise.reject(new Error("synthetic"))]);\n  console.log(outcomes.map(x => x.status).join(","));\n})().catch(error => { console.error(error.message); process.exitCode = 1; });','["profile","count"]\nfulfilled,rejected','Observe input-order aggregation and explicit partial-failure statuses.',['Start independent synthetic promises.','Await all and inspect order independently of completion speed.','Use allSettled for a separate declared partial-outcome example.'],'The fixture establishes aggregation semantics only, not real service capacity, network latency or remote cancellation.'),mistakes:['Assuming Promise.all cancels every other operation on rejection.','Starting unbounded parallel requests without capacity limits.'],takeaways:['Use dependency, capacity and failure semantics to choose scheduling.','Make partial success and stale-result handling explicit.'],quiz:[
['What order does Promise.all use for its fulfillment values?','Input order','Always completion order','Alphabetical value order','Random scheduler order','Promise.all preserves the positions of its supplied inputs in the fulfilled result.','Faster completion does not reorder result positions.','Values are not sorted alphabetically.','Ordering is defined rather than random.'],
['Does Promise.all automatically cancel other started work on rejection?','No','Yes, every side effect is undone','Only if arrays are short','Only if the first value is a string','Rejection of the aggregate does not itself cancel other operations or reverse effects.','Undo requires an actual operation contract.','Input count does not supply cancellation.','Payload type is unrelated.'],
['Which helper reports each supplied promise’s settlement?','Promise.allSettled','Only JSON.stringify','Only Array.filter','Only Number.isFinite','AllSettled returns status records for all input settlements.','Serialization does not await outcomes.','Filtering does not aggregate promise settlement.','Numeric checks are unrelated.'],
['A second request requires the first request’s new id. What is appropriate?','Sequence that dependency','Start with a random id','Ignore the first rejection','Always use all without inputs','The required identifier must be available before the dependent operation can be correct.','Random data is not the created identity.','Failure affects whether the next operation is valid.','Aggregation cannot invent missing input.'],
['A dashboard starts 10,000 independent requests at once. What needs review?','Concurrency and service capacity','Only the array’s variable name','Only the button colour','Only the title metadata','Independent operations can still overload finite resources and need limits.','Names do not limit load.','Colours do not constrain requests.','Metadata does not set capacity.'],
['A partial-results view uses allSettled. What must the UI still explain?','Which required data failed','Only that a function returned','Only the response array length','Only a generic success banner','Partial success requires honest state communication and safety decisions for missing information.','Return alone does not mean useful completion.','Length does not explain failures.','A blanket success message can mislead.'],
['An old request finishes after a new filter selection. What should be checked?','Result relevance to current state','Only its completion timestamp','Only the row’s text length','Only the package version','Completion alone does not make a result current for the active view.','Latest arrival can still be stale.','Length does not identify the selected query.','Version is not request relevance.'],
['The aggregate fixture returns profile before count. What was not established?','Profile completed first','Input order was preserved','Both values fulfilled','The array had two entries','Promise.all result order does not reveal completion order.','That is the defined observed behavior.','Fulfillment values were available.','Two inputs were supplied.']
]},
{id:'fetch-status-contract',comp:'m4.c1',title:'Check fetch status before interpreting the body',introduction:'A customer page calls fetch for an order summary. A fulfilled fetch promise can still contain a 404 or 500 response, so success handling must inspect the actual response contract rather than equating promise fulfillment with business success.',sections:[s('Transport and HTTP results differ','Fetch can reject for network-related failures or cancellation, while HTTP error statuses normally arrive as Response objects. Check response.ok or the expected status range and interpret special cases according to the endpoint contract. A 204 response has no content, so unconditionally parsing JSON can fail.','Response.json reads and parses the body asynchronously and can reject for invalid JSON. Bodies are streams and are generally consumable once; use a clone deliberately if multiple independent readers are truly needed. Do not log private response bodies just to simplify debugging.'),s('Cancellation and request state need policy','AbortController can signal cancellation to supported operations, but it does not guarantee reversal of a remote mutation already performed. Credentials and CORS behavior must follow the application’s browser/server policy; client options do not bypass server access checks. A failed status needs a useful safe UI state, not invented order data.','The fixture constructs actual Response objects in Node, exercising status and JSON reading without contacting a network service. It does not prove browser fetch CORS, cookie, redirect or abort behavior. Those need real browser fixtures under controlled origins.')],worked:w('(async () => {\n  const missing = new Response("not found", {status: 404});\n  console.log(missing.ok, missing.status);\n  const success = new Response(JSON.stringify({count: 2}), {status: 200, headers: {"Content-Type": "application/json"}});\n  console.log((await success.json()).count);\n})().catch(error => { console.error(error.message); process.exitCode = 1; });','false 404\n2','Inspect actual local Response status and parse a separate JSON body.',['Construct a 404 response and inspect ok/status.','Construct a successful JSON response.','Await its body parsing without claiming a network request occurred.'],'The fixture tests Response APIs on supplied data only. It does not contact an API or verify browser network/security behavior.'),mistakes:['Treating any fulfilled fetch as a successful HTTP operation.','Parsing every response as JSON even when the contract has no body.'],takeaways:['Separate transport, status and representation parsing.','Keep cancellation, credentials and UI outcomes explicit.'],quiz:[
['Does a fulfilled fetch promise guarantee status 200?','No, inspect the response status','Yes, every 4xx rejects automatically','Yes, every 5xx rejects automatically','Only if JSON is expected','HTTP error statuses can still fulfill with a Response object.','Fetch does not automatically reject every 4xx.','The same applies to 5xx statuses.','Expected content type does not change that rule.'],
['What does response.ok normally indicate?','Status in the 200-299 range','Every business invariant passed','Every JSON field is valid','The user owns every record','Ok reflects the HTTP success status range rather than complete business validity.','Business rules need their own evidence.','Schema validation is separate.','Authorization is independent.'],
['What can response.json do?','Parse the body asynchronously','Guarantee JSON is valid without reading','Return a synchronous object always','Authorize the server operation','Json returns a promise for body consumption and parsing and can fail.','Malformed JSON can reject.','The result is asynchronous.','Parsing is not authorization.'],
['An endpoint returns 204 and no body. What should the client avoid?','Unconditional JSON parsing','Checking the documented contract','Handling empty success','Inspecting the status','A no-content response need not have a JSON representation to parse.','Contract checking is appropriate.','That can be the expected outcome.','Status inspection is useful.'],
['A 500 response arrives with HTML while the client expects JSON. What must be handled?','Status and parsing','Only CSS layout','Only the logo colour','Only the user’s timezone','Status handling and parsing assumptions should match the actual response contract.','Layout does not parse bodies.','Colours do not define media types.','Timezone does not correct this mismatch.'],
['A response body is consumed twice without cloning. What needs review?','Body stream consumption','Only the JSON indentation','Only the variable name','Only the request path length','Response bodies are generally single-consumption streams under the API contract.','Whitespace does not reset a stream.','Names do not make it reusable.','Length is unrelated.'],
['An abort signal is sent after a remote payment was accepted. What should not be assumed?','The payment was undone','Local waiting can end','The operation needs reconciliation policy','The remote state may be uncertain','Cancellation of local fetch does not guarantee reversal of remote effects.','That can happen locally.','Reconciliation may be necessary.','Uncertainty is part of the operation contract.'],
['The Response fixture reports false 404. What did it not test?','Actual network fetch behavior','The supplied status value','The response.ok property','A local Response construction','The response was constructed in memory rather than retrieved through browser networking.','Status was explicit input.','The property is read.','Construction is executed.']
]},
{id:'storage-scope-lifecycle',comp:'m4.c2',title:'Choose localStorage and sessionStorage by lifecycle',introduction:'A dashboard saves a preferred theme and a temporary draft. Storage mechanisms differ in lifetime and sharing; neither should become an unreviewed vault for sensitive credentials or another user’s private records.',sections:[s('Web Storage stores strings under an origin','LocalStorage and sessionStorage expose synchronous string-key/string-value storage in browsers. LocalStorage commonly persists across sessions until cleared or otherwise managed by browser policy. SessionStorage is scoped to an origin and a page session/top-level browsing context, with nuances such as initial copying when a new context has an opener. It is not simply a universally shared short-lived localStorage.','Storage can fail because of quota, privacy policy, blocked access or unavailable contexts. Serialization can fail or parsed data can be malformed/obsolete, so version and validate stored records. Storage operations are synchronous and excessive large writes can harm responsiveness.'),s('Separate convenience from authority','A stored theme is a convenience preference, not evidence of account permission. Do not make a protected page accessible merely because localStorage says role=admin. Browser-accessible storage can be read by scripts running with that origin’s capabilities; security policy must consider XSS and credential lifetime rather than assuming persistence equals safety.','The fixture uses a Map as an explicitly labeled storage model and JSON for string serialization. It does not execute browser localStorage/sessionStorage, origin isolation, quotas or tab lifecycle. Browser fixtures are required for those guarantees.')],worked:w('const storageModel = new Map();\nstorageModel.set("preferences", JSON.stringify({version: 1, theme: "teal"}));\nconst parsed = JSON.parse(storageModel.get("preferences"));\nconsole.log(parsed.version, parsed.theme);\nconsole.log(typeof storageModel.get("preferences"));','1 teal\nstring','Serialize a versioned preference in a clearly bounded string-storage model.',['Construct an in-memory Map model.','Store JSON text rather than an implicit object reference.','Parse the text and inspect its version and theme.'],'This is not a browser Web Storage implementation and proves no origin, persistence, quota or multi-tab behavior.'),mistakes:['Treating localStorage as an authorization source.','Assuming storage writes always succeed.'],takeaways:['Choose lifecycle and scope deliberately.','Version, validate and protect browser-stored data.'],quiz:[
['What type of values does Web Storage directly store?','Strings','Only deep object graphs','Only DOM nodes','Only promises','Web Storage key/value interfaces use strings, so structured values need deliberate serialization.','Objects are not stored as arbitrary live graphs.','Nodes are not preserved as live DOM values.','Promises are not persisted as asynchronous state.'],
['Which is a reasonable localStorage use under a suitable policy?','A nonsecret theme preference','A universal server admin grant','Every private customer record','A raw credential by default','Convenience preferences can fit, while authority and sensitive-data decisions need separate controls.','Client storage cannot grant server permission.','Bulk private data needs strong justification and protection.','Credential storage requires a deliberate security policy.'],
['Are Web Storage operations asynchronous promises?','No, they are synchronous APIs','Yes, every call awaits a network','Only for arrays','Only when JSON is used','LocalStorage/sessionStorage operations are synchronous and can affect main-thread work.','They do not inherently await network calls.','Input type does not choose scheduling.','JSON does not change the API to promises.'],
['A saved preference record changes schema in a new release. What helps?','Versioning and validation','Only a new CSS border','Only a shorter key','Only ignoring parse errors','Stored data can outlive code versions and needs safe compatibility handling.','Styling does not migrate data.','Key length alone is insufficient.','Ignoring errors can hide corruption or stale schema.'],
['A storage write throws due to quota. What should the application do?','Handle the failure deliberately','Assume every value was saved','Disable all server validation','Delete unrelated user records','Storage availability is not guaranteed and the UI should not falsely report persistence.','A thrown write is not success.','Server validation is independent.','Unrelated deletion is inappropriate.'],
['A developer says sessionStorage is shared across every tab indefinitely. What is wrong?','Its scope/lifecycle is different','All browser storage is forbidden','SessionStorage is always a database','JSON guarantees that statement','SessionStorage has page-session/context boundaries and does not have that universal sharing/lifetime.','Storage can be useful under appropriate policy.','It is a browser API, not a server database.','Serialization does not define tab scope.'],
['A page trusts stored role=admin. What must the backend still verify?','Server permission','Only the key’s spelling','Only the stored JSON syntax','Only the browser theme','Client-controlled state is not authoritative access-control evidence.','Names do not establish trust.','Valid JSON can contain false claims.','Preferences do not authorize operations.'],
['The Map fixture reads version 1 and teal. What remains unverified?','Browser storage scope','The supplied JSON text','The parsed object fields','The value’s string type','An in-memory Map does not implement browser storage policy or lifetime.','The text is explicit input.','The fields are parsed.','The type is printed.']
]},
{id:'storage-safe-recovery',comp:'m4.c2',title:'Recover from stale, malformed or account-specific browser state',introduction:'A shared workstation signs out one student and signs in another. Saved drafts and preferences need clear ownership rules so a convenient resume feature does not expose the previous student’s private work.',sections:[s('Validate identity and schema at read time','Use account-aware keys or an equivalent ownership design when storing account-specific state, and clear or detach it according to the logout/account-switch policy. A key containing a user id is still client data, so the server must independently authorize any resumed operation. Avoid restoring a draft before confirming which account is active.','Treat stored JSON as untrusted and possibly stale. Catch parse failures, validate version and required field types, and decide whether to migrate, ignore or safely remove unsupported data. Do not turn malformed storage into a fake completed assessment or overwrite verified server progress.'),s('Keep persistence evidence separate from server truth','A successful local setItem does not mean the server saved a roadmap, and a failed local write does not erase previously verified server evidence. Distinguish local convenience state from authoritative assessment and enrollment records. Storage events and cross-context behavior have specific contracts; do not assume every write notifies the same page synchronously.','The fixture parses two synthetic stored records and accepts only the supported version for the expected account. It tests that local validation policy with no actual browser storage. Account-switch, tab-event and persistence behavior still need browser and application integration evidence.')],worked:w('function restore(text, expectedUser) {\n  try { const value = JSON.parse(text); return value.version === 1 && value.user === expectedUser && typeof value.lesson === "string" ? value.lesson : null; }\n  catch { return null; }\n}\nconsole.log(restore(JSON.stringify({version: 1, user: 7, lesson: "intro"}), 7));\nconsole.log(restore(JSON.stringify({version: 1, user: 8, lesson: "private"}), 7));\nconsole.log(restore("not-json", 7));','intro\nnull\nnull','Restore only supported synthetic state belonging to the expected account.',['Parse through a guarded boundary.','Check version, owner and field type.','Reject mismatched and malformed records without inventing progress.'],'The policy is local and narrow. It does not implement actual storage, server authority or complete migration handling.'),mistakes:['Restoring the previous account’s draft before confirming identity.','Treating locally stored completion flags as verified assessment evidence.'],takeaways:['Validate version, owner and shape before restoration.','Keep convenience persistence separate from authoritative progress.'],quiz:[
['What should account-specific saved state identify?','Its intended account ownership','Only the logo filename','Only the page background','Only the browser’s window size','Ownership helps avoid restoring one person’s private state for another account.','Branding does not identify owner.','Colours do not bind state to an account.','Window size is not identity.'],
['Can stored JSON be assumed valid forever?','No, validate on reading','Yes, serialization freezes the schema','Only when the key is short','Only when a theme is selected','Storage can contain malformed, stale or modified data and needs validation.','Serialization does not prevent later incompatibility.','Length does not establish validity.','Preferences do not validate other records.'],
['What should local completion flags not replace?','Verified server evidence','A nonsecret layout preference','A temporary scroll position','An optional draft display hint','Authoritative scores and validated progress must not be invented from client convenience state.','Preferences can be local under policy.','Scroll state can be a convenience value.','Draft hints can be local when appropriate.'],
['A shared workstation switches accounts while a draft restore is pending. What must be checked?','Current account identity','Only the old draft’s length','Only the stored colour','Only the file extension','A delayed restore must not cross the account boundary and expose or apply another person’s state.','Length does not prove ownership.','Colour is not identity.','Extensions do not bind user state.'],
['A record has an unsupported schema version. What is appropriate?','A deliberate migrate/ignore policy','Always treat it as completed work','Always copy every field blindly','Always assign it to the newest user','Compatibility handling must preserve truth and ownership rather than invent progress or trust arbitrary fields.','Completion needs real evidence.','Blind copying can misinterpret stale data.','Reassigning ownership can expose private state.'],
['A local preference save succeeds. What does it not prove?','The server saved a roadmap','The local API accepted that write','The application attempted persistence','A local convenience value existed','Local and server persistence are separate operations with separate evidence.','That can be scoped local evidence.','The call can be observed.','The stored value can be local state.'],
['A storage event is expected to update the same page immediately after every write. What should be reviewed?','The actual browser event contract','Only the key’s capitalization','Only JSON whitespace','Only the navigation colour','Storage notifications have specific context and timing semantics, not that universal guarantee.','Names do not define event delivery.','Formatting does not choose notification behavior.','Colours are unrelated.'],
['The restore fixture rejects user 8 for expected user 7. What is still required?','Account-switch UI tests','The explicit owner comparison','The supported version condition','The malformed JSON catch','A local function check does not exercise asynchronous UI restoration, actual storage or server authorization.','That comparison is executed.','That version check is present.','That error path is tested.']
]}
];
